#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
OpenCode session inventory in a GTK window, with archiving on click.

The desktop app's own archive button is compiled out of the renderer
(`d8=!1` in app.asar gates it), and the plugin's slash commands only render
to a synthetic message the desktop app may never display. This reads the same
database and provides a window that works either way.

ARCHIVING LIVES HERE, not in OpenCode. The plugin's `/archive_Put` and
`/archive_Pull` commands were removed so there is only one archive path: right-
click a session, then press Archive on it, or Restore if it is already
archived. The desktop app cannot offer this either -- its archive button is
compiled out of the renderer (`d8=!1` in app.asar gates it).

RIGHT-CLICKING A ROW OPENS ITS BUTTONS, and the strip carries its own Close,
which does nothing but put it away. Opening a strip used to be one click with
three ways out that were all undiscoverable from the strip itself: Escape, a
second click on the same row, or clicking some other row. Close is the exit
that is visible from where you are.

The buttons on every row, archived or not:

  Archive / Restore  the primary action, and the one the section heading
                     promises. Disabled on the live session, and refused by
                     the write path too.
  Transcript.md      the whole conversation as Markdown, to
                     ~/Downloads/<title>.md. For reading.
  Export.json       OpenCode's own export format, to ~/Downloads/<title>.json,
                     importable with `opencode session import`. The only route
                     that leaves this machine: archive and restore are direct
                     writes to this database and exist nowhere else.
  Delete             removes the session and its messages, after a dialog that
                     defaults to Cancel and offers Archive instead. Also
                     refused for the live session.
  Close              nothing at all.

DELETING is the one action here with no way back from this window, so it is
the only one that asks. A snapshot is taken before it, as for every write --
but only the last three snapshots are kept, so a delete is close to
irreversible, and the tooltip says so.

THE LIST IS GROUPED BY STATE, which is what tells you which action applies:

  Unarchived sessions first, then archived ones, each under a heading naming
  the action that row offers. An archived session cannot be archived again and
  a working one cannot be restored, so the grouping is the instruction.

Rows are sorted by session name within each section. Rows are unnumbered: every
action here is a click, so a number in front of a row was a position the user
had to remember and could not use. Archiving moves a session between sections
anyway, so a number would have been stale the moment it was read.

WRITES follow the plugin's safety model from src/db.ts:

  - A `VACUUM INTO` snapshot is taken before any write, which is consistent
    even while OpenCode holds the database open in WAL mode.
  - Only `time_archived` and `time_updated` are ever written. No message row
    is touched, on either side of the operation.
  - Snapshots are capped, so the backups directory cannot grow without bound.
  - Writes are wrapped in `BEGIN IMMEDIATE` with a busy timeout, and a failure
    rolls back rather than leaving a half-applied change.
  - Restoring also refreshes `time_updated`, which is required: OpenCode sorts
    by that column and pages per directory, so clearing the flag alone leaves
    the session buried where it cannot be reached.

The database is opened read-write. Archiving and restoring touch only
`time_archived` and `time_updated`, and no message row is touched on either
side of that operation. Deleting is the exception: it removes the session row
and every table keyed by `session_id`, in one transaction.

  session-list-popup              the window
  session-list-popup --copy       print the plain-text list to stdout
  session-list-popup --db PATH    use a different database (used by the tests)
"""

